## Discussion

Both abortion care access and female genital mutilation exhibit strong statistical associations with maternal mortality rates. Highly restrictive abortion legislation and cultural acceptance of FGC/M compound vulnerability—for example, Chad exhibits an abortion score of 3, an FGC/M score of 5, and an MMR of 1,140 per 100,000 live births. Conversely, nations with fewer than 10 deaths per 100,000 live births consistently maintain low restriction scores ($\le 2$).

While these findings are significant, maternal mortality is multifaceted. Other confounding factors include prenatal care access, nutrition, baseline poverty, maternal age at first birth, parity, and contraceptive access (Women Still Face Big Gaps in Access to Health Care, 2013; Eyeberu et al., 2022). Furthermore, cross-sectional analysis in a single year (2020) limits longitudinal causal inference across reporting agencies.

Nonetheless, policy and cultural factors remain directly actionable. Investing in expanded reproductive care access and enforcing protections against FGC/M are foundational steps toward improving maternal survivability and overall population health (Grossi, 2024).

